{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '1090,1115p;1320,1352p;1390,1475p;1928,1960p;1980,2006p;2058,2075p;2145,2180p;2228,2260p'\"","aggregated_output":"  1090\t  by lift and lower. The `linked` transition is four acts: acts 1\n  1091\t  and 2 run once, at `validated`; acts 3 and 4 run once per\n  1092\t  receiving task -- the parent at `linked`, and each waiter or later\n  1093\t  requirer at its own restore.\n  1094\t  1. *The child's export slice is lifted* to UCF's portable encoding\n  1095\t     (section 7.5.1, amended below): each closure becomes a\n  1096\t     `:yin.k/closure` marker naming its **origin image** and its entry\n  1097\t     relative to that image, with its captured values encoded\n  1098\t     recursively; every other value encodes by the same grammar. The\n  1099\t     origin is not assumed to be the module's own image: a closure the\n  1100\t     child obtained from a dependency and re-exported originates in\n  1101\t     that dependency's image, and the lift records whichever image the\n  1102\t     closure's coordinates fall in. The lift is refused as\n  1103\t     `:yin.k/non-portable` for the same leaves UCF refuses (a host\n  1104\t     object, an in-memory handle), and a refused lift refuses the\n  1105\t     install: an export that cannot cross a boundary is not an export.\n  1106\t  2. *The origin set is checked.* The lifted slice's\n  1107\t     `:yin.k/requires :yin.k/segments` enumerates every origin image it\n  1108\t     names, transitively through captured values. Each must be an image\n  1109\t     this scheduler verified: the module's own, or one delivered for an\n  1110\t     install in this scheduler's history (held under `:installs` or the\n  1111\t     registry by identity). An origin identity the scheduler never\n  1112\t     verified is `:foreign-image`, and the install is refused; a\n  1113\t     verified image is never re-fetched for this step.\n  1114\t  3. *The receiving task attaches every origin image its kernel does\n  1115\t     not yet hold*, non-destructively. The ordinary loaders\n  1320\t  and fall through to its primitives and module registry -- where\n  1321\t  `x` is unbound or bound to something the publisher never saw. The\n  1322\t  stores therefore cross with the exports, under UCF section 7.6.2's\n  1323\t  slice discipline:\n  1324\t  - *Lift.* One snapshot is encoded per module, not just the child's\n  1325\t    own: the child's halted store under its manifest address, and\n  1326\t    for every dependency whose store the exports reach transitively\n  1327\t    (a re-exported closure carries that dependency's `:store-of`),\n  1328\t    that dependency's store instance as it stands in the child's\n  1329\t    state at halt -- including mutations the child made through the\n  1330\t    dependency's own closures, which a snapshot of the child's store\n  1331\t    alone would not carry. A `:store-of` with no snapshot is a lift\n  1332\t    refusal, `:yin.k/non-portable` with\n  1333\t    `:yin.k/kind :missing-module-store`. Each snapshot encodes as a\n  1334\t    `:yin.k/store` slice (UCF 7.6.2); whatever that grammar refuses\n  1335\t    (a host object in the store; engine resources cannot be, by the\n  1336\t    private-resource rule below) refuses the lift and the install.\n  1337\t  - *Marker.* Every closure marker names the store its body resolves\n  1338\t    against, `:yin.k/store-of m` with `m` the module's manifest\n  1339\t    address -- an amendment to UCF section 7.5.1 listed in section\n  1340\t    11, item 12. The snapshots travel once per slice, not per\n  1341\t    closure.\n  1342\t  - *Lower.* Each receiving task instantiates every snapshot into\n  1343\t    `:module-stores {m {sym value}}` in its own state, one instance\n  1344\t    per module: a store the task already holds stands as\n  1345\t    instantiated -- first link wins, and a later slice never\n  1346\t    overwrites a live instance -- while snapshots it lacks are\n  1347\t    instantiated fresh, so a task sees exactly one store per module.\n  1348\t    Nothing is written into any task's ambient store: this is\n  1349\t    7.6.2's \"there is no merge\" rule at the module grain -- a\n  1350\t    receiver binding of the same name can neither supply nor shadow\n  1351\t    the module's value.\n  1352\t  - *Run time.* While a closure carrying `:store-of m` executes, the\n  1390\t  `[:store-get :stream-0]` and read a host handle no publisher\n  1391\t  handed it. The split is therefore physical, not conventional:\n  1392\t  stream handles, cursor cells, and the FFI pair live in a private\n  1393\t  `:resources` table in VM state; the engine's own machinery\n  1394\t  (wait-set resolution, handle creation and polling, resume) reads\n  1395\t  and writes only that table; and no user store instruction and no\n  1396\t  `resolve-var` step consults it, at top level or inside a module\n  1397\t  closure. The ids may stay predictable -- they name nothing a\n  1398\t  store instruction can reach. This amends the store model UCF\n  1399\t  section 7.6.2 itself states -- that section opens by listing what\n  1400\t  the store S holds (listed with the other amendments in section\n  1401\t  11, item 12; r8's citation of a \"UCF 4.1\" named no such section,\n  1402\t  corrected r10): a migration slice still carries resource\n  1403\t  cells, remapped at resume, but beside the program store, never\n  1404\t  inside it.\n  1405\t\n  1406\t  **Resource lowering (r9).** The continuation format's own decode\n  1407\t  rules move with the split, or the private table is bypassed at\n  1408\t  every boundary crossing: UCF section 7.5.1's table decodes a\n  1409\t  `:yin.k/stream` marker to \"an attached handle, under a fresh\n  1410\t  store key\" and a `:yin.k/cursor-ref` to \"a store cursor entry\",\n  1411\t  and section 7.5.3 lowers each logical cell to \"a fresh store\n  1412\t  cursor entry\". From r9 those decode targets are the private\n  1413\t  table, as an amendment to UCF sections 7.5.1 and 7.5.3 (added to\n  1414\t  the list in section 11, item 12): lowering installs an attached\n  1415\t  handle under a fresh **resource id** in `:resources`, lowers a\n  1416\t  logical cell to a cursor entry in `:resources` seeded with its\n  1417\t  carried position, and remaps every `:yin.k/cursor-ref` to that\n  1418\t  entry; two refs to one cell still share one entry. Program\n  1419\t  values keep only the non-forgeable references -- the opaque\n  1420\t  `stream-ref` and `cursor-ref` ids, exactly as a running program\n  1421\t  holds them -- and no store key is ever created. A module export\n  1422\t  carrying a stream or cursor reference therefore lifts and lowers\n  1423\t  through the same rules, the lift authenticating the reference\n  1424\t  first as the sealed-references rule below requires: the marker\n  1425\t  crosses in the slice, the\n  1426\t  receiver's `:resources` gains the attachment or cell, and the\n  1427\t  export's value in the receiver is the remapped reference,\n  1428\t  indistinguishable from one the receiver made itself.\n  1429\t\n  1430\t  **Sealed references (r10).** r9 moved resources out of the store,\n  1431\t  but a reference is still a plain map with a predictable id\n  1432\t  (`{:type :stream-ref, :id :stream-0}`), and an effect carries\n  1433\t  whatever value the program supplies: a program or linked module\n  1434\t  can write the literal itself and name a resource it was never\n  1435\t  handed -- another task's stream, a cursor cell, or the FFI pair's\n  1436\t  ids. Shape checks are not the answer (UCF 7.5.1 refuses every\n  1437\t  \"looks like data\" rule); unguessability is. Each task's resources\n  1438\t  are bound to a **task-scoped capability secret**, minted once by\n  1439\t  the composition at task creation from its own random source and\n  1440\t  held in VM state -- never in a program value, never on a stream,\n  1441\t  never counter-derived. Every reference the engine issues -- a\n  1442\t  `stream-ref`, a `cursor-ref`, an FFI cell id -- carries a seal\n  1443\t  over its id under that secret, and every effect dispatch that\n  1444\t  resolves a program-supplied reference verifies the seal against\n  1445\t  the active task's secret before touching `:resources`. A literal\n  1446\t  with a wrong or missing seal fails closed with\n  1447\t  `:forged-resource-reference`, naming the effect and the id it\n  1448\t  named: the effect boundary, not the store instruction, was the\n  1449\t  last read path, and r10 closes it. Lift and lower re-seal rather\n  1450\t  than carry, and **lift authenticates before it encodes** (r11):\n  1451\t  stripping a seal unexamined would launder one, because r10\n  1452\t  verified seals only at dispatch -- a module could export a\n  1453\t  fabricated reference literal that never passed an effect in the\n  1454\t  child, and a lift that merely dropped the seal would emit a\n  1455\t  portable marker the lower then mints into a valid,\n  1456\t  receiver-sealed capability. From r11 the lift, before emitting\n  1457\t  a `:yin.k/stream` or `:yin.k/cursor-ref` marker, verifies the\n  1458\t  reference's seal and resource kind against the emitter task's\n  1459\t  own capability secret; an invalid, unsealed, or forged reference\n  1460\t  refuses the export lift immediately, as `:yin.k/non-portable`\n  1461\t  with `:yin.k/kind :forged-resource-reference`. Only an\n  1462\t  authenticated reference is encoded without its seal (the marker\n  1463\t  is the portable encoding), and the lower, having installed the\n  1464\t  attachment or cell in the receiver's `:resources`, issues a\n  1465\t  fresh reference sealed under the receiver's secret -- which is\n  1466\t  what r9's \"remapped reference\" now means. One task's literals\n  1467\t  cannot guess another task's secret, so cross-task forgery and\n  1468\t  the export-laundering path fail with the same refusal. This\n  1469\t  amends UCF section 7.5.1's reference markers (added to the list\n  1470\t  in section 11, item 12).\n  1471\t  Executing exports inside the owning child instead was considered and\n  1472\t  rejected: it keeps a child VM alive for the parent's lifetime, and\n  1473\t  every application of an export becomes a function call across a VM\n  1474\t  boundary, which is the coupling the axioms forbid. Relocation costs\n  1475\t  one lift and one lower per install and nothing afterwards, and it is\n  1928\tbody applied before its definition retains its obligation and is\n  1929\t`:undeclared-free` unless the manifest declares it; an export-only\n  1930\timage with no application site produces no obligations; a parked\n  1931\tentry recorded before an attach restores after it at the same pcs\n  1932\twith `:segment` never assigned from the entry; a register call in\n  1933\tflight while a nested require attaches an image returns into the\n  1934\tgrown code space, the attachment intact after the return; two tasks\n  1935\tholding images of different lengths require the same module and each\n  1936\tapplies its export correctly; a `:store-put` executed inside an\n  1937\texported closure writes the module store, and a module closure\n  1938\tcalling another module's closure writes each module's own store; a\n  1939\tslice re-exporting a dependency closure carries the dependency's\n  1940\tstore snapshot with the child's mutations, and a task already\n  1941\tholding that dependency's store keeps its own instance; a module\n  1942\tforging a predictable resource key (`[:store-get :stream-0]`)\n  1943\tagainst a task with a live stream reads nothing on any backend; a\n  1944\tforged reference literal -- stream, cursor, or FFI cell id --\n  1945\tpassed to an effect is `:forged-resource-reference` while the\n  1946\ttask's own sealed references resolve, and a lifted-then-lowered\n  1947\treference works